284
00:08:38,500 --> 00:08:39,880
and continue executing from there.

285
00:08:39,880 --> 00:08:41,500
That's the execution process of a function.

286
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:43,760
Now, during this function execution process,

287
00:08:43,760 --> 00:08:45,340
what concepts do we know are involved?

288
00:08:45,340 --> 00:08:47,200
For example, we say the caller

289
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:48,280
may need to pass

290
00:08:48,280 --> 00:08:49,600
arguments to the callee,

291
00:08:49,600 --> 00:08:50,400
right?

292
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:52,400
And when the caller

293
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:53,340
returns,

294
00:08:53,340 --> 00:08:55,000
it may need to return some content,

295
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:56,440
which we call the return value.

296
00:08:56,440 --> 00:08:59,020
There's also a return address involved,

297
00:08:59,020 --> 00:08:59,720
these concepts.

298
00:08:59,720 --> 00:09:03,480
So what do all these concepts involve?

299
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:04,220
They involve, that is to say,

300
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:06,340
the interaction between the caller function and the callee.

301
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:07,600
So it's essentially a

302
00:09:07,600 --> 00:09:09,960
there's a boundary here

303
00:09:09,960 --> 00:09:11,140
between the two

304
00:09:11,140 --> 00:09:13,080
there has to be some kind of

305
00:09:13,080 --> 00:09:15,620
where do I put my call arguments

306
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:18,660
that is, where does my caller put them

307
00:09:18,660 --> 00:09:19,760
and only then can this callee go

308
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:20,900
and actually get those arguments

309
00:09:20,900 --> 00:09:21,500
think about it, right?

310
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:23,360
When I return,

311
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:24,700
this callee has to put the return value

312
00:09:24,700 --> 00:09:25,640
somewhere too

313
00:09:25,640 --> 00:09:26,460
and then,

314
00:09:26,460 --> 00:09:27,360
the caller also has to know

315
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:28,840
and once the caller knows,

316
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:29,920
it can go and take this data

317
00:09:29,920 --> 00:09:30,440
and pull it out

318
00:09:30,440 --> 00:09:31,920
So all of these things

319
00:09:31,920 --> 00:09:32,900
are really just a kind of

320
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:35,360
agreement that both sides have to settle on

321
00:09:35,360 --> 00:09:36,020
Think about it —

322
00:09:36,020 --> 00:09:38,000
if we don't agree on this beforehand,

323
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:40,200
the whole process would fall apart.

324
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:42,680
Now some of you might say,

325
00:09:42,680 --> 00:09:44,280
isn't this simple enough?

326
00:09:44,280 --> 00:09:46,440
I can put it wherever I want.

327
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:48,060
Like in the example we just had,

328
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:50,740
we put the function's return address

329
00:09:50,740 --> 00:09:52,560
right in x5.

330
00:09:52,560 --> 00:09:52,900
Right?

331
00:09:52,900 --> 00:09:53,980
You still remember, right?

332
00:09:53,980 --> 00:09:57,740
In the example we just had,

333
00:09:57,740 --> 00:09:59,580
yes, this very example.

334
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:03,020
In this example,

335
00:10:03,020 --> 00:10:04,240
what we actually did was make a convention.

336
00:10:04,240 --> 00:10:05,560
What did we do, and how did we agree on it?

337
00:10:05,560 --> 00:10:07,840
We took that return address

338
00:10:07,840 --> 00:10:09,540
and agreed that it would be placed in x5, right?

339
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:11,860
So what is this function actually?

340
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:12,500
It's the caller.

341
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:15,580
This is the caller

342
00:10:15,580 --> 00:10:17,160
And this sum is what?

343
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:17,740
It's the callee

344
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:21,020
Right?

345
00:10:21,020 --> 00:10:23,160
So the two sides actually reach an agreement

346
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:25,420
We put the return address in X5

347
00:10:25,420 --> 00:10:25,940
That way

348
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:27,980
the flow works on both sides

349
00:10:27,980 --> 00:10:28,460
Think about it, doesn't it?

350
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:29,700
So

351
00:10:29,700 --> 00:10:31,720
if that's the case

352
00:10:31,720 --> 00:10:32,700
why do we even need an agreement?

353
00:10:32,700 --> 00:10:33,600
We could just decide on our own

354
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:34,740
If I feel like using X5, that's fine

355
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:36,300
Today I feel like using X5

356
00:10:36,300 --> 00:10:37,460
Tomorrow I'll use S6, and that's fine too

357
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:38,000
Think about it, doesn't it?

358
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,500
Right?

359
00:10:39,500 --> 00:10:41,660
Well, this is definitely not as simple as you think

360
00:10:41,660 --> 00:10:43,260
Why is that? Think about it

361
00:10:43,260 --> 00:10:44,800
So where does the problem come from?

362
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:48,440
Well,

363
00:10:48,440 --> 00:10:52,100
if the caller and the callee

364
00:10:52,100 --> 00:10:53,020
are both written by you,

365
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:55,980
then of course there's no problem at all.

366
00:10:55,980 --> 00:10:57,340
You don't need any convention whatsoever.

367
00:10:57,340 --> 00:10:59,340
You don't need an agreement with yourself.

368
00:10:59,340 --> 00:10:59,860
Think about it, right?

369
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:03,060
But in most cases,

370
00:11:03,060 --> 00:11:06,220
the function you write may be used by someone else,

371
00:11:06,220 --> 00:11:06,880
right?

372
00:11:06,880 --> 00:11:09,200
That means the caller and the callee

373
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:10,360
may not be the same person.

374
00:11:10,360 --> 00:11:14,020
And that's when you need a convention.

375
00:11:14,100 --> 00:11:15,200
You see what I mean?

376
00:11:15,200 --> 00:11:17,900
Everyone has to follow one uniform rule

377
00:11:17,900 --> 00:11:19,520
for where you put your parameters,

378
00:11:19,520 --> 00:11:20,560
where you put your return value,

379
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:21,960
and where you put your return address.

380
00:11:21,960 --> 00:11:23,740
And this way,

381
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:26,020
The function you write is handed over for others to use,

382
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:28,180
so that other people can use it too.

383
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:29,760
Similarly, when the compiler

384
00:11:29,760 --> 00:11:30,660
generates machine code,

385
00:11:30,660 --> 00:11:33,160
it also has to follow this convention when generating where your

386
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:34,920
call parameters and return values are placed,

387
00:11:34,920 --> 00:11:36,320
and how return parameters are handled.

388
00:11:36,320 --> 00:11:37,620
This is a kind of convention.

389
00:11:37,620 --> 00:11:41,680
So, as part of our standard,

390
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:42,720
in our assembly,

391
00:11:42,720 --> 00:11:45,820
apart from defining these assembly instructions,

392
00:11:45,820 --> 00:11:49,160
we also define this part about the calling convention.

393
00:11:49,160 --> 00:11:50,420
This is also part of the specification.

394
00:11:50,420 --> 00:11:55,380
As for this whole programming convention,

395
00:11:55,380 --> 00:11:57,300
as we will see later,

396
00:11:57,300 --> 00:12:00,740
we can briefly summarize it into about three parts.

397
00:12:00,740 --> 00:12:04,360
One is the programming convention for registers,

398
00:12:04,360 --> 00:12:06,180
that is, how we use these registers.

399
00:12:06,180 --> 00:12:07,020
We have 32

400
00:12:07,020 --> 00:12:09,660
— for example, RISC-V has 32 registers.

401
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:11,300
you can't just use them however you like

402
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:14,680
This is the programming convention for registers.

403
00:12:14,680 --> 00:12:16,060
The second one

404
00:12:16,060 --> 00:12:16,580
is about

405
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:19,880
the programming convention for function call and return instructions.

406
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:22,180
That is, when we do function calls,

407
00:12:22,180 --> 00:12:24,280
the most critical parts are a function call instruction

408
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:26,480
and a function return instruction.

409
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:29,120
How we should use these two instructions

410
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:30,520
also follows conventions,

411
00:12:30,520 --> 00:12:31,440
and there are requirements for them too.

412
00:12:31,440 --> 00:12:32,160
That's the second point.

413
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:33,420
The third point is this:

414
00:12:33,420 --> 00:12:34,900
when we implement a function,

415
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:36,780
say I implement a function for others to use,

416
00:12:36,780 --> 00:12:39,740
there are also requirements for how that function is implemented.

417
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:40,640
What this function

418
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:42,100
should do,

419
00:12:42,100 --> 00:12:42,720
certain aspects of it,

420
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:44,340
we have specific requirements for.

421
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:44,880
So,

422
00:12:44,880 --> 00:12:46,560
once we've grasped these three parts,

423
00:12:46,560 --> 00:12:47,380
basically

424
00:12:47,380 --> 00:12:49,280
the programming convention part

425
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:50,920
will be clear to us.

426
00:12:50,920 --> 00:12:51,760
Let's take a look.

427
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:52,600
First, let's look at part one,

428
00:12:52,600 --> 00:12:53,980
which is about

429
00:12:53,980 --> 00:12:56,060
the programming conventions for registers.

430
00:12:56,060 --> 00:12:58,560
Let's take a look at this table.

431
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:00,480
This table,

432
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:01,500
that is to say,

433
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,800
first, what is the first concept?

434
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:04,620
First, as we know,

435
00:13:04,620 --> 00:13:06,880
our registers,

436
00:13:06,880 --> 00:13:07,700
in RISC-V,

437
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:08,760
the general-purpose registers,

438
00:13:08,760 --> 00:13:10,580
there are 32 of them in total.

439
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:12,020
Now, what did we talk about earlier?

440
00:13:12,020 --> 00:13:13,260
x0, x1, x2

441
00:13:13,260 --> 00:13:14,840
This is one kind of it.

442
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:15,940
In other words, in the spec,

443
00:13:15,940 --> 00:13:17,380
the name defined for this register,

444
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:18,980
think of it as a standardized name.

445
00:13:18,980 --> 00:13:21,300
You can think of it as its real name.

446
00:13:21,300 --> 00:13:22,720
For example, my real name is Wang Chen,

447
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:23,400
right?

448
00:13:23,400 --> 00:13:24,680
That's my real name.

449
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:26,780
This is the real name defined in the spec.

450
00:13:26,780 --> 00:13:28,820
But when we program,

451
00:13:28,820 --> 00:13:30,440
since we have this concept of convention,

452
00:13:30,440 --> 00:13:32,600
because we often use this register,

453
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:33,280
to do a certain thing,

454
00:13:33,280 --> 00:13:34,560
we've gotten used to,

455
00:13:34,560 --> 00:13:36,280
not using numbers to represent it,

456
00:13:36,280 --> 00:13:38,080
because numbers don't convey any meaning.

457
00:13:38,080 --> 00:13:39,700
We give it an alias.

458
00:13:39,700 --> 00:13:41,100
This alias,

459
00:13:41,100 --> 00:13:42,600
is a name with a certain meaning.

460
00:13:42,600 --> 00:13:43,600
Do you get what I mean?

461
00:13:43,600 --> 00:13:46,160
Of course, it seems I don't have any aliases yet.

462
00:13:46,160 --> 00:13:47,580
This lets me give it a nickname.

463
00:13:47,580 --> 00:13:49,180
I can give it an alias.

464
00:13:49,180 --> 00:13:50,360
This is called an alias.

465
00:13:50,360 --> 00:13:52,900
And this is called the programming name.

466
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:54,140
That is, when we're programming,

467
00:13:54,140 --> 00:13:55,880
we actually habitually write this name.

468
00:13:55,880 --> 00:13:57,320
That is to say, in your code,

469
00:13:57,320 --> 00:13:57,960
for example, this one,

470
00:13:57,960 --> 00:14:00,120
this X0 register,

471
00:14:00,120 --> 00:14:01,160
it has an alias called zero.

472
00:14:01,160 --> 00:14:02,620
So when you write code,

473
00:14:02,620 --> 00:14:04,720
writing either of these two — the assembler recognizes both.

474
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:05,840
Everyone gets what I mean, right?

475
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:06,500
It recognizes both.

476
00:14:06,500 --> 00:14:07,440
But,

477
00:14:07,440 --> 00:14:08,860
more often, perhaps,

478
00:14:08,860 --> 00:14:10,680
sometimes when you look at code written by other people,

479
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:13,300
more people prefer to use this name zero,

480
00:14:13,300 --> 00:14:15,600
simply because it has a specific meaning in this context.

481
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:17,680
So why does x0 express a certain meaning?

482
00:14:17,680 --> 00:14:19,460
When you read it, it's always 0.

483
00:14:19,460 --> 00:14:21,340
Writing into it is completely meaningless.

484
00:14:21,340 --> 00:14:22,280
You see what I mean.

485
00:14:22,280 --> 00:14:23,860
So we give it a real

486
00:14:23,860 --> 00:14:26,240
common name, calling it zero,

487
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:27,060
to represent it.

488
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:27,620
That's the idea.

489
00:14:27,620 --> 00:14:29,560
When we write programs,

490
00:14:29,560 --> 00:14:33,100
I actually recommend using these programming names,

491
00:14:33,100 --> 00:14:34,620
because it's easier to understand that way.

492
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:36,860
Writing those numbers instead is harder to follow.

493
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:38,460
Keep that in mind.

494
00:14:38,460 --> 00:14:41,860
So these are their programming names.

495
00:14:41,860 --> 00:14:43,580
As for exactly why they're called these names,

496
00:14:43,580 --> 00:14:45,140
for example, why it's called ra,

497
00:14:45,140 --> 00:14:46,360
of course, a lot of them are abbreviations.

498
00:14:46,360 --> 00:14:49,360
Later, when we go through the conventions and explain them,

499
00:14:49,360 --> 00:14:51,260
we'll get a deeper understanding of why they're named this way.

500
00:14:51,260 --> 00:14:53,480
And there's one more thing to understand

501
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:55,840
That is, who is responsible during a function call

502
00:14:55,840 --> 00:14:58,280
for maintaining these registers.

503
00:14:58,280 --> 00:15:00,360
So when we go through the introduction,

504
00:15:00,360 --> 00:15:01,820
we'll walk you through that as well.

505
00:15:01,820 --> 00:15:03,000
Let's first take a look at

506
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,420
the first one, X0.

507
00:15:04,420 --> 00:15:05,500
We already talked about X0 just now,

508
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:07,020
so we won't go into it.

509
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:07,900
Because it's very special,

510
00:15:07,900 --> 00:15:09,700
we gave it a name, zero.

511
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:11,920
Now, it

512
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:13,060
doesn't really have a specific use

513
00:15:13,060 --> 00:15:14,780
during function calls,

514
00:15:14,780 --> 00:15:16,200
so we don't consider

515
00:15:16,200 --> 00:15:18,080
the issue of freely maintaining this register

516
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:19,760
during function calls for it.

517
00:15:19,760 --> 00:15:22,100
So this NA here means

518
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:23,900
not considered,

519
00:15:23,900 --> 00:15:24,560
not relevant,

520
00:15:24,560 --> 00:15:25,340
Not available.

521
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:26,140
That's what it means.

522
00:15:26,140 --> 00:15:28,500
Let's go over it.

523
00:15:28,500 --> 00:15:29,240
Okay.

524
00:15:29,240 --> 00:15:31,840
So the next register is a very important one.

525
00:15:31,840 --> 00:15:33,100
This register, x1,

526
00:15:33,100 --> 00:15:35,940
its alias is ra.

527
00:15:35,940 --> 00:15:40,680
We say it's actually used to hold the function's

528
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:42,340
This wording doesn't seem quite accurate.

529
00:15:42,340 --> 00:15:44,040
It's not called storing the return

530
00:15:44,040 --> 00:15:46,520
It should be storing the value of the function's return address.

531
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:47,680
Please note this.

532
00:15:47,680 --> 00:15:49,660
You can go back and correct this.

533
00:15:49,660 --> 00:15:53,280
It should be storing the return value of the function address

534
00:15:53,280 --> 00:15:54,080
the value of the address.

535
00:15:54,080 --> 00:15:56,180
Storing the return value of the function address.

536
00:15:56,180 --> 00:15:58,400
In English, we call it the return address,

537
00:15:58,400 --> 00:16:01,900
which actually expresses the meaning more accurately.

538
00:16:01,900 --> 00:16:05,860
So ra is actually the abbreviation for return address.

539
00:16:05,940 --> 00:16:08,100
ra, an abbreviation for return address.

540
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:10,020
It's actually what we just talked about

541
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:11,720
when that function returns,

542
00:16:11,720 --> 00:16:13,760
the next address it is going to execute,

543
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:15,580
the address specified as the next one.

544
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:16,580
Do you still get what I mean?

545
00:16:16,580 --> 00:16:19,080
So,

546
00:16:19,080 --> 00:16:22,720
why do we call it caller here?

547
00:16:22,720 --> 00:16:24,260
It means that,

548
00:16:24,260 --> 00:16:26,060
it says that,

549
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:27,980
it is the person who calls the function,

550
00:16:27,980 --> 00:16:29,240
who maintains this

551
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:31,240
content of this register.

552
00:16:31,240 --> 00:16:32,420
So what does that mean?

553
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:33,140
It means that,

554
00:16:33,140 --> 00:16:34,740
when, say, function A

555
00:16:34,740 --> 00:16:35,800
calls function B,

556
00:16:35,800 --> 00:16:37,400
this function B is the callee.

557
00:16:37,400 --> 00:16:38,020
Think about it, isn't that right?

558
00:16:38,020 --> 00:16:40,120
That just shows that the callee may, inside

559
00:16:40,120 --> 00:16:41,680
its own function,

560
00:16:41,680 --> 00:16:43,800
change the value of this ra.

561
00:16:43,800 --> 00:16:46,300
So this callee,

562
00:16:46,300 --> 00:16:47,780
this function A,

563
00:16:47,780 --> 00:16:49,280
will need to,

564
00:16:49,280 --> 00:16:51,440
say if sometimes it might

565
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:55,360
possibly, this callee

566
00:16:55,360 --> 00:16:56,400
might want to use this value,

567
00:16:56,400 --> 00:16:57,760
then you'll have to figure out a way

568
00:16:57,760 --> 00:16:58,600
to save this value yourself.

569
00:16:58,600 --> 00:16:59,700
So,

570
00:16:59,700 --> 00:17:01,040
we define it as

571
00:17:01,040 --> 00:17:03,440
the callee being responsible for maintaining

572
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:04,980
the value in this register.

573
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:07,160
We'll come back to this concept

574
00:17:07,160 --> 00:17:08,760
when we talk about the assembly code

575
00:17:08,760 --> 00:17:10,520
and look at it again then.

576
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:12,540
You'll probably understand it more deeply by then.

577
00:17:12,540 --> 00:17:14,420
For now, let's just remember this conclusion.

578
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:17,380
Alright, so the second one is called x2.

579
00:17:17,380 --> 00:17:18,120
As for x2,

580
00:17:18,120 --> 00:17:19,960
we call it sp.

581
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:21,700
What does SP mean?

582
00:17:21,700 --> 00:17:22,660
It stands for Stack Pointer.

583
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:24,340
That's the stack pointer we talked about earlier.

584
00:17:24,340 --> 00:17:26,380
When we push onto and pop off the stack,

585
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:27,800
we're essentially moving this stack pointer.

586
00:17:27,800 --> 00:17:30,180
So we often use this register

587
00:17:30,180 --> 00:17:31,180
to hold the stack pointer.

588
00:17:31,180 --> 00:17:31,880
That's why,

589
00:17:31,880 --> 00:17:35,080
we named it SP.

590
00:17:35,080 --> 00:17:38,500
Now, who is responsible for maintaining it?

591
00:17:38,500 --> 00:17:40,320
We say it's the callee function,

592
00:17:40,320 --> 00:17:44,000
meaning that inside the called sub-function,

593
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,600
we maintain this stack.

594
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:47,180
Because as we know, popping and pushing

595
00:17:47,180 --> 00:17:49,400
are actually done inside the sub-function.

596
00:17:49,400 --> 00:17:51,960
In a moment, again through examples,

597
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:53,580
we'll gain a deeper understanding of this concept.

598
00:17:53,580 --> 00:17:55,980
Next up,

599
00:17:55,980 --> 00:17:58,280
there's a group of registers,

600
00:17:58,280 --> 00:18:00,580
namely x5 to x7.

601
00:18:00,580 --> 00:18:01,860
from x8 to x31

602
00:18:01,860 --> 00:18:03,820
how many registers is that in total?

603
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:05,200
5 to 7

604
00:18:05,200 --> 00:18:06,120
5, 6, 7, that's 3 of them

605
00:18:06,120 --> 00:18:08,480
28, 29, 30, 31

606
00:18:08,480 --> 00:18:09,100
that's 4 of them

607
00:18:09,100 --> 00:18:10,520
4 plus 3 gives us 7 in total

608
00:18:10,520 --> 00:18:12,160
these 7 registers

609
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:13,700
we call them

610
00:18:13,700 --> 00:18:15,980
temporary registers

611
00:18:15,980 --> 00:18:17,460
so the ones starting with t

612
00:18:17,460 --> 00:18:18,860
so the registers here

613
00:18:18,860 --> 00:18:20,640
their alias is t

614
00:18:20,640 --> 00:18:21,880
t followed by a number

615
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:24,740
now this t

616
00:18:24,740 --> 00:18:26,660
we say it actually is

617
00:18:26,660 --> 00:18:30,560
you can think of it this way

618
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:31,980
these temporary registers

619
00:18:31,980 --> 00:18:35,320
temporary relative to whom?

620
00:18:35,320 --> 00:18:39,040
they are temporary relative to the caller

621
00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:40,980
That is to say, for these registers,

622
00:18:40,980 --> 00:18:44,040
in this calling convention,

623
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:46,220
when function A calls function B,

624
00:18:46,220 --> 00:18:47,760
for example, when it calls function B,

625
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:49,500
inside function B,

626
00:18:49,500 --> 00:18:51,220
it's basically our convention that

627
00:18:51,220 --> 00:18:52,320
in this function B,

628
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:55,820
you're allowed to freely use these temporary registers.

629
00:18:55,820 --> 00:18:58,260
Now, suppose this function A

630
00:18:58,260 --> 00:19:00,160
also uses these t registers,

631
00:19:00,160 --> 00:19:01,980
because once it enters this function,

632
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:03,400
these registers might get overwritten.

633
00:19:03,400 --> 00:19:04,740
You see what I mean, right?

634
00:19:04,740 --> 00:19:07,020
So that means, if my function A

635
00:19:07,020 --> 00:19:09,820
uses these registers internally,

636
00:19:09,820 --> 00:19:11,280
then before you call the function,

637
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:12,460
you need to take these registers yourself,

638
00:19:12,460 --> 00:19:17,020
and before you make the call as the caller,

639
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:18,400
you need to save their values.

640
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:22,160
And then, when this function returns,

641
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:24,660
You just need to restore them yourself and that's it.

642
00:19:24,660 --> 00:19:26,560
That is to say, these temporary registers,

643
00:19:26,560 --> 00:19:28,640
in calls, in these functions,

644
00:19:28,640 --> 00:19:29,640
will get overwritten.

645
00:19:29,640 --> 00:19:31,120
That's what's meant by temporary registers.

646
00:19:31,120 --> 00:19:33,900
So what does this so-called saving

647
00:19:33,900 --> 00:19:34,780
mean, think about it.

648
00:19:34,780 --> 00:19:37,600
Where do we save them to?

649
00:19:37,600 --> 00:19:40,180
Where else

650
00:19:40,180 --> 00:19:41,680
can we use to hold register values?

651
00:19:41,680 --> 00:19:44,620
Well, of course they're still saved in the function stack.

652
00:19:44,620 --> 00:19:45,160
Right?

653
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:47,140
Saved in whose function stack?

654
00:19:47,140 --> 00:19:49,580
Well, of course they're saved by the caller,

655
00:19:49,580 --> 00:19:50,900
since the caller does the saving,

656
00:19:50,900 --> 00:19:52,760
then the caller saves them in its own stack.

657
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:56,900
So that's the idea of temporary registers.

658
00:19:56,900 --> 00:19:59,240
Next,

659
00:19:59,240 --> 00:20:01,900
we have what are called saved registers.

660
00:20:01,900 --> 00:20:03,140
As for saved registers,

661
00:20:03,140 --> 00:20:04,360
It's the opposite of that one

662
00:20:04,360 --> 00:20:06,400
It's called S saved

663
00:20:06,400 --> 00:20:07,040
Saved means

664
00:20:07,040 --> 00:20:08,280
These all start with S

665
00:20:08,280 --> 00:20:09,800
So for these registers

666
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:14,140
it's the caller who is responsible for maintaining them

667
00:20:14,140 --> 00:20:15,940
So what that means is

668
00:20:15,940 --> 00:20:16,940
when you call

669
00:20:16,940 --> 00:20:18,260
say this function A

670
00:20:18,260 --> 00:20:19,780
again, let's say A calls B

671
00:20:19,780 --> 00:20:21,560
A calls B

672
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:25,580
Suppose A uses this register S0

673
00:20:25,580 --> 00:20:26,460
Let me give an example

674
00:20:26,460 --> 00:20:27,960
Say it uses the register S0

675
00:20:27,960 --> 00:20:30,840
So when A calls B

676
00:20:30,840 --> 00:20:32,600
actually, B

677
00:20:32,600 --> 00:20:35,080
can always be guaranteed that

678
00:20:35,080 --> 00:20:36,400
when B returns

679
00:20:36,400 --> 00:20:39,280
the value in S0 is unchanged

680
00:20:39,280 --> 00:20:41,000
That's what we call a saved register

681
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:42,960
So, since everyone thinks

682
00:20:42,960 --> 00:20:44,940
if B wants to guarantee that the value in S0

683
00:20:44,940 --> 00:20:47,900
stays the same before and after the call,

684
00:20:47,900 --> 00:20:49,640
then one approach is this:

685
00:20:49,640 --> 00:20:52,320
you just never use this S0 register inside B.

686
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:52,900
Does that sound right?

687
00:20:52,900 --> 00:20:55,060
But that's impossible,

688
00:20:55,060 --> 00:20:57,300
because you might still need to use this S0 register.

689
00:20:57,300 --> 00:20:58,380
There are only so many registers—

690
00:20:58,380 --> 00:20:59,840
thirty-something might not be enough.

691
00:20:59,840 --> 00:21:01,460
So if you do need to use it, what can you do?

692
00:21:01,460 --> 00:21:04,380
What does B have to do?

693
00:21:04,380 --> 00:21:05,740
Since it's the one responsible for preserving it,

694
00:21:05,740 --> 00:21:07,640
then you find a way to, before using it,

695
00:21:07,640 --> 00:21:10,500
save this S0 register onto the stack first,

696
00:21:10,500 --> 00:21:12,180
and then start using S0.

697
00:21:12,180 --> 00:21:14,020
After you're done with it, before returning,

698
00:21:14,020 --> 00:21:15,360
there's one more thing you have to do—what is it?

699
00:21:15,360 --> 00:21:18,900
You restore this S0 back from the stack.

700
00:21:18,900 --> 00:21:19,940
Think about it—does that make sense?

701
00:21:19,940 --> 00:21:21,180
restore it.

702
00:21:21,180 --> 00:21:22,600
So after it's restored,

703
00:21:22,600 --> 00:21:23,640
when we return,

704
00:21:23,640 --> 00:21:24,820
from A's perspective,

705
00:21:24,820 --> 00:21:25,900
from A's point of view,

706
00:21:25,900 --> 00:21:27,260
the value of s0 hasn't changed at all, right?

707
00:21:27,260 --> 00:21:28,360
Think about it — isn't that the idea?

708
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:29,740
This is what's called

709
00:21:29,740 --> 00:21:31,880
So for the s registers,

710
00:21:31,880 --> 00:21:35,260
it's the callee that guarantees their values are preserved.

711
00:21:35,260 --> 00:21:37,920
So make sure you understand this,

712
00:21:37,920 --> 00:21:38,600
that's the concept.

713
00:21:38,600 --> 00:21:41,740
Compared with the t registers, it's basically the opposite arrangement.

714
00:21:41,740 --> 00:21:46,540
Then there are also the argument registers,

715
00:21:46,540 --> 00:21:47,440
the argument registers,

716
00:21:47,440 --> 00:21:50,380
meaning the arguments.

717
00:21:50,380 --> 00:21:51,540
So these registers,

718
00:21:51,540 --> 00:21:52,800
they all start with a,

719
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:54,140
they're used when we pass parameters,

720
00:21:54,140 --> 00:21:55,560
when calling a function, we need to pass parameters.

721
00:21:55,560 --> 00:21:57,800
So for RISC-V,

722
00:21:57,800 --> 00:21:58,920
it recommends that you

723
00:21:58,920 --> 00:22:00,300
just pass the parameters directly in the registers,

724
00:22:00,300 --> 00:22:00,860
passing them through the registers

725
00:22:00,860 --> 00:22:02,260
because that's the most efficient,

726
00:22:02,260 --> 00:22:03,180
the fastest way.

727
00:22:03,180 --> 00:22:03,980
So that's why we

728
00:22:03,980 --> 00:22:06,240
use a0 through a7,

729
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:07,220
which adds up to

730
00:22:07,220 --> 00:22:08,400
8 registers you can use.

731
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:10,220
In other words, you can fully make use of

732
00:22:10,220 --> 00:22:12,300
8 registers to pass parameters.

733
00:22:12,300 --> 00:22:14,240
Then when returning,

734
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:16,220
the return value can also be placed

735
00:22:16,220 --> 00:22:18,120
into the two registers a0 and a1.

736
00:22:18,120 --> 00:22:19,100
That's what it means.

737
00:22:19,100 --> 00:22:21,640
Now similarly,

738
00:22:21,640 --> 00:22:23,360
since these registers are used for passing parameters,

739
00:22:23,360 --> 00:22:24,260
that means

740
00:22:24,260 --> 00:22:28,900
for the caller,

741
00:22:28,900 --> 00:22:30,760
Actually, the value in this register

742
00:22:30,760 --> 00:22:32,040
might get changed.

743
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:33,400
So if you want to

744
00:22:33,400 --> 00:22:35,040
use it again after using it once,

745
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:35,960
you have to maintain it yourself.

746
00:22:35,960 --> 00:22:38,080
So it is maintained by the caller.

747
00:22:38,080 --> 00:22:40,940
Now, through this convention,

748
00:22:40,940 --> 00:22:42,280
what advantage can you see?

749
00:22:42,280 --> 00:22:43,720
I'm essentially saying that

750
00:22:43,720 --> 00:22:45,420
some registers are volatile before the call,

751
00:22:45,420 --> 00:22:46,200
they can change,

752
00:22:46,200 --> 00:22:46,900
while some registers

753
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:48,180
stay unchanged after the call,

754
00:22:48,180 --> 00:22:48,500
right?

755
00:22:48,500 --> 00:22:49,040
So

756
00:22:49,040 --> 00:22:50,520
the point is to achieve

757
00:22:50,520 --> 00:22:51,080
what kind of effect?

758
00:22:51,080 --> 00:22:52,100
If you didn't have this rule,

759
00:22:52,100 --> 00:22:53,520
to guarantee that this register

760
00:22:53,520 --> 00:22:54,300
is preserved during use,

761
00:22:54,300 --> 00:22:55,120
always

762
00:22:55,120 --> 00:22:56,600
and never get disturbed

763
00:22:56,600 --> 00:22:57,980
one of the dumbest approaches would be this:

764
00:22:57,980 --> 00:22:59,700
when function A

765
00:22:59,700 --> 00:23:00,600
calls function B,

766
00:23:00,600 --> 00:23:01,620
I save every single register,

767
00:23:01,620 --> 00:23:03,020
all of them,

768
00:23:03,020 --> 00:23:03,540
right? Of course, whether that's the way...

769
00:23:03,540 --> 00:23:04,780
and then,

770
00:23:04,780 --> 00:23:05,380
and then,

771
00:23:05,380 --> 00:23:06,160
when exiting the function,

772
00:23:06,160 --> 00:23:07,000
I restore all those registers

773
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:07,920
back out again.

774
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:09,320
Actually, this can also achieve

775
00:23:09,320 --> 00:23:10,240
the same effect.

776
00:23:10,240 --> 00:23:10,860
But as you all know,

777
00:23:10,860 --> 00:23:11,960
the efficiency is far too low,

778
00:23:11,960 --> 00:23:13,180
because we have so many registers.

779
00:23:13,180 --> 00:23:14,500
If every time you call a function,

780
00:23:14,500 --> 00:23:15,680
you save all the registers...

781
00:23:15,680 --> 00:23:17,220
and restore them again on the way out

782
00:23:17,220 --> 00:23:18,200
that is way too inefficient

783
00:23:18,200 --> 00:23:18,760
Think about it, isn't it

784
00:23:18,760 --> 00:23:20,540
So we simply settle on a convention

785
00:23:20,540 --> 00:23:21,900
that is, there is a set of registers

786
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:24,240
that you don't have to worry about

787
00:23:24,240 --> 00:23:25,620
and some registers that you must handle

788
00:23:25,620 --> 00:23:26,440
And that way

789
00:23:26,440 --> 00:23:29,060
we improve the efficiency of

790
00:23:29,060 --> 00:23:30,760
function calls as much as possible

791
00:23:30,760 --> 00:23:31,460
Similarly

792
00:23:31,460 --> 00:23:32,800
with this standard in place

793
00:23:32,800 --> 00:23:33,760
we also reached what we talked about earlier

794
00:23:33,760 --> 00:23:35,600
we solved the problem we mentioned earlier

795
00:23:35,600 --> 00:23:37,240
namely, the generality of functions

796
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:38,160
Because this way

797
00:23:38,160 --> 00:23:39,420
as long as everyone follows this convention

798
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:40,200
a function of yours

799
00:23:40,200 --> 00:23:41,760
whether you wrote it or I wrote it

800
00:23:41,760 --> 00:23:42,920
can be handed over to anyone to use

801
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:45,240
So if we all follow this convention,

802
00:23:45,240 --> 00:23:45,920
things won't get messy.

803
00:23:45,920 --> 00:23:48,800
So this register convention,

804
00:23:48,800 --> 00:23:49,960
that's basically the idea.

805
00:23:49,960 --> 00:23:52,960
And now you also understand

806
00:23:52,960 --> 00:23:54,940
why these names start with a,

807
00:23:54,940 --> 00:23:56,340
why some start with s and some with t.

808
00:23:56,340 --> 00:23:58,660
They actually carry meaning.

809
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:00,960
Later, when we write code,

810
00:24:00,960 --> 00:24:05,380
we'll try to use these programming names as much as possible.

811
00:24:05,380 --> 00:24:07,220
They are also called ABI names.

812
00:24:07,220 --> 00:24:08,540
As for the ABI,

813
00:24:08,540 --> 00:24:09,320
the concept,

814
00:24:09,320 --> 00:24:10,120
just get a general understanding of it.

815
00:24:10,120 --> 00:24:11,660
I won't go into it in detail here.

816
00:24:11,660 --> 00:24:13,960
Alright,

817
00:24:13,960 --> 00:24:16,420
so first of all, the first thing we mentioned is,

818
00:24:16,420 --> 00:24:18,780
the first aspect of the convention is the register convention.

819
00:24:18,780 --> 00:24:19,920
The second aspect,

820
00:24:19,920 --> 00:24:22,880
is what happens when we talk about function calls.

821
00:24:22,880 --> 00:24:25,200
When it comes to function jumps and returns,

822
00:24:25,200 --> 00:24:26,760
in terms of instruction encoding,

823
00:24:26,760 --> 00:24:28,620
actually, combining this with what we just talked about,

824
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:29,880
the convention on these registers,

825
00:24:29,880 --> 00:24:30,800
we can do the same thing here,

826
00:24:30,800 --> 00:24:33,580
and define part of the convention for its call instructions as well.

827
00:24:33,580 --> 00:24:35,700
Let's take a look at what this convention is.

828
00:24:35,700 --> 00:24:37,260
Actually, we've already covered some of it earlier.

829
00:24:37,260 --> 00:24:38,960
For example,

830
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:40,000
as you can see,

831
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:41,300
this actually involves some pseudo-instructions.

832
00:24:41,300 --> 00:24:44,720
Because pseudo-instructions were also proposed

833
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:47,300
for the sake of making things more convenient for us to use.

834
00:24:47,300 --> 00:24:48,980
You see,

835
00:24:48,980 --> 00:24:50,400
instructions like this JAR instruction,

836
00:24:50,400 --> 00:24:53,240
it is actually a pseudo-instruction.

837
00:24:53,240 --> 00:24:54,220
It actually uses

838
00:24:54,220 --> 00:24:55,440
the register convention we defined earlier.

839
00:24:55,440 --> 00:24:57,240
Didn't we just agree that x1

840
00:24:57,240 --> 00:24:59,220
Didn't we just agree on X1?

841
00:24:59,220 --> 00:25:03,400
That's what we call ra, right?

842
00:25:03,400 --> 00:25:03,960
Think about it, isn't that so?

843
00:25:03,960 --> 00:25:07,140
So we actually agreed on X1 as the convention

844
00:25:07,140 --> 00:25:10,060
to save the return address

845
00:25:10,060 --> 00:25:11,800
and we'll never just use it casually again

846
00:25:11,800 --> 00:25:12,820
Unlike before, when we used it however we liked

847
00:25:12,820 --> 00:25:13,580
Before we had this convention,

848
00:25:13,580 --> 00:25:14,480
we used X5,

849
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:14,840
right?

850
00:25:14,840 --> 00:25:15,660
In that example.

851
00:25:15,660 --> 00:25:16,480
Now, what I'm saying is,

852
00:25:16,480 --> 00:25:18,380
since we've all agreed to use X1,

853
00:25:18,380 --> 00:25:19,300
in our code,

854
00:25:19,300 --> 00:25:21,340
we shouldn't keep writing X1 explicitly every single time.

855
00:25:21,340 --> 00:25:22,600
So we defined a

856
00:25:22,600 --> 00:25:23,580
pseudo-instruction, the ret instruction.

857
00:25:23,580 --> 00:25:24,520
For this instruction,

858
00:25:24,520 --> 00:25:26,180
notice that it doesn't specify

859
00:25:26,180 --> 00:25:28,000
an rd here.

860
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:29,420
If rd isn't specified,

861
00:25:29,420 --> 00:25:30,680
So what's actually implied

862
00:25:30,680 --> 00:25:31,540
is the use of x1.

863
00:25:31,540 --> 00:25:32,860
So from now on, when we code,

864
00:25:32,860 --> 00:25:33,620
when we write our programs,

865
00:25:33,620 --> 00:25:35,020
we should try to use the call instruction,

866
00:25:35,020 --> 00:25:37,760
which by convention uses x1

867
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:38,860
to save the return address.

868
00:25:38,860 --> 00:25:40,960
That's one point.

869
00:25:40,960 --> 00:25:42,720
The second one is this:

870
00:25:42,720 --> 00:25:43,900
as we just mentioned,

871
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:44,700
this kind is called

872
00:25:44,700 --> 00:25:46,580
a non-returning function call.

873
00:25:46,580 --> 00:25:48,340
That is, as we just agreed,

874
00:25:48,340 --> 00:25:49,220
x0

875
00:25:49,220 --> 00:25:50,140
is zero,

876
00:25:50,140 --> 00:25:51,540
a special address,

877
00:25:51,540 --> 00:25:52,780
a special register.

878
00:25:52,780 --> 00:25:53,720
So as long as you put

879
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:55,620
the return address in there, it can't actually hold

880
00:25:55,620 --> 00:25:56,760
any return address at all.

881
00:25:56,760 --> 00:25:58,180
So since that's the case,

882
00:25:58,180 --> 00:25:59,040
from now on we'll just

883
00:25:59,040 --> 00:26:00,980
not write it out so long and wordy.

884
00:26:00,980 --> 00:26:02,400
We'll just write a J.

885
00:26:02,400 --> 00:26:04,900
JR just represents this kind of

886
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:06,400
equivalent effect.

887
00:26:06,400 --> 00:26:08,380
That's the second type.

888
00:26:08,380 --> 00:26:11,060
Then there's also a third type,

889
00:26:11,060 --> 00:26:12,900
which is — didn't we just say

890
00:26:12,900 --> 00:26:14,980
we have some long jumps?

891
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:16,200
You remember, right?

892
00:26:16,200 --> 00:26:18,040
So for long jumps,

893
00:26:18,040 --> 00:26:19,280
because long jumps,

894
00:26:19,280 --> 00:26:22,560
long jumps mainly use these instructions.

895
00:26:22,560 --> 00:26:24,560
And because a long jump

896
00:26:24,560 --> 00:26:27,380
itself also implicitly uses X1,

897
00:26:27,380 --> 00:26:29,440
or rather, the tail call.

898
00:26:29,440 --> 00:26:31,380
A tail call — you can imagine

899
00:26:31,380 --> 00:26:32,240
what a tail call is.

900
00:26:32,240 --> 00:26:33,980
A tail call is, in English, called a tail

901
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:37,620
Say function A calls function B,

902
00:26:37,620 --> 00:26:39,420
and function B calls function C.

903
00:26:39,420 --> 00:26:42,360
Then this function C doesn't call any function.

904
00:26:42,360 --> 00:26:43,100
So in the end, this

905
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:45,520
step where B calls C,

906
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:47,320
we call that a tail call.

907
00:26:47,320 --> 00:26:49,520
You get what I mean, right?

908
00:26:49,520 --> 00:26:51,900
In English, it's usually called tail

909
00:26:51,900 --> 00:26:53,560
— basically the last call.

910
00:26:53,560 --> 00:26:56,000
Because this function C can never go on to call function D.

911
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:56,800
You get what I mean.

912
00:26:56,800 --> 00:26:59,100
So this kind is called a tail call.

913
00:26:59,100 --> 00:27:01,340
Now what about A calling B?

914
00:27:01,340 --> 00:27:02,320
That's not a tail call,

915
00:27:02,320 --> 00:27:03,700
because after it calls B,

916
00:27:03,700 --> 00:27:04,680
B will still go on to call C.

917
00:27:04,680 --> 00:27:05,000
That kind of thing.

918
00:27:05,000 --> 00:27:07,100
So that step is not called a tail call.

919
00:27:07,100 --> 00:27:08,100
That's called a regular call.

920
00:27:08,100 --> 00:27:10,260
So that's what a tail call means.

921
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:11,340
A pseudo call means that

922
00:27:11,340 --> 00:27:13,860
it can no longer save this

923
00:27:13,860 --> 00:27:16,080
because after B calls C,

924
00:27:16,080 --> 00:27:18,860
C will just

925
00:27:18,860 --> 00:27:20,300
C will just

926
00:27:20,300 --> 00:27:22,260
Right,

927
00:27:22,260 --> 00:27:24,360
it won't go on to call D anymore.

928
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:25,980
So we don't need to save this

929
00:27:25,980 --> 00:27:27,600
final return address again.

930
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:30,440
So here we can just use x0.

931
00:27:30,440 --> 00:27:33,460
Okay, that's

932
00:27:33,460 --> 00:27:36,620
another group of

933
00:27:36,620 --> 00:27:37,600
commonly used ones.

934
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:38,520
Now the last one

935
00:27:38,520 --> 00:27:40,220
concerns returning from a function.

936
00:27:40,220 --> 00:27:41,540
Because we also agreed that

937
00:27:41,540 --> 00:27:44,820
x1 holds this return address,

938
00:27:44,820 --> 00:27:45,660
so we don't need to

939
00:27:45,660 --> 00:27:47,020
write it out in such a long-winded way.

940
00:27:47,020 --> 00:27:48,200
We can just use one

941
00:27:48,200 --> 00:27:49,980
Simply put, the pseudo-instruction

942
00:27:49,980 --> 00:27:51,260
ret is the return instruction,

943
00:27:51,260 --> 00:27:53,200
used to indicate a return.

944
00:27:53,200 --> 00:27:55,540
Of course, this is not the return of the C language.

945
00:27:55,540 --> 00:27:58,900
This is the return pseudo-instruction in assembly.

946
00:27:58,900 --> 00:28:00,240
Now, this kind of thing

947
00:28:00,240 --> 00:28:02,140
involves function jumps,

948
00:28:02,140 --> 00:28:03,060
and it's a programming convention.

949
00:28:03,060 --> 00:28:04,300
That is, from now on,

950
00:28:04,300 --> 00:28:05,200
we should try our best to use this

951
00:28:05,200 --> 00:28:06,900
set of instructions

952
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:10,500
to write function calls and returns.

953
00:28:10,500 --> 00:28:12,560
Just so you're aware of this.

954
00:28:12,560 --> 00:28:17,610
Now, the ones we use most often

955
00:28:17,610 --> 00:28:18,770
are call and return,

956
00:28:18,770 --> 00:28:20,350
which are used a lot.

957
00:28:20,350 --> 00:28:24,460
That's it.

958
00:28:24,460 --> 00:28:26,280
Now, regarding this thing,

959
00:28:26,280 --> 00:28:28,380
let's take another look at the effect.

960
00:28:28,380 --> 00:28:30,120
We've made a simple animated diagram for you.

961
00:28:30,120 --> 00:28:31,520
Let's take a look at how this works.

962
00:28:31,520 --> 00:28:32,540
For example, suppose we now have

963
00:28:32,540 --> 00:28:34,600
the caller on the left

964
00:28:34,600 --> 00:28:36,440
and the callee on the right.

965
00:28:36,440 --> 00:28:38,760
Let's look at this function process,

966
00:28:38,760 --> 00:28:39,760
and take another look at this.

967
00:28:39,760 --> 00:28:42,260
That is to say, once this programming convention is in place,

968
00:28:42,260 --> 00:28:43,360
the function's invocation

969
00:28:43,360 --> 00:28:45,700
how does the function call actually take place?

970
00:28:45,700 --> 00:28:47,680
Suppose our caller

971
00:28:47,680 --> 00:28:50,400
starts executing instruction i,

972
00:28:50,400 --> 00:28:54,720
then moves down to i+1, the second instruction,

973
00:28:54,720 --> 00:28:56,340
and then further down,

974
00:28:56,340 --> 00:28:58,160
this instruction starts making a call.

975
00:28:58,160 --> 00:28:59,500
Now we know that this call

976
00:28:59,500 --> 00:29:00,740
already has a specific meaning.

977
00:29:00,740 --> 00:29:01,760
According to our programming convention,

978
00:29:01,760 --> 00:29:02,600
what is it equivalent to?

979
00:29:02,600 --> 00:29:04,920
Think back to what we just covered,

980
00:29:04,920 --> 00:29:07,600
it is actually equivalent to a long jump.

981
00:29:07,600 --> 00:29:08,980
As for this long jump,

982
00:29:08,980 --> 00:29:10,700
we don't care about AUIPC.

983
00:29:10,700 --> 00:29:12,380
What we mainly care about is the second instruction below,

984
00:29:12,380 --> 00:29:13,220
which is JLR.

985
00:29:13,220 --> 00:29:14,380
As for JLR,

986
00:29:14,380 --> 00:29:15,400
its target,

987
00:29:15,400 --> 00:29:17,000
its return address is,

988
00:29:17,000 --> 00:29:19,380
by convention, saved in x1.

989
00:29:19,380 --> 00:29:19,920
Think about it, isn't that right?

990
00:29:19,920 --> 00:29:21,860
Now, x1,

991
00:29:21,860 --> 00:29:23,600
as we said, has an alias called ra.

992
00:29:23,600 --> 00:29:24,920
So what it does inside is,

993
00:29:24,920 --> 00:29:26,160
what operation does it perform?

994
00:29:26,160 --> 00:29:28,360
It takes its next address,

995
00:29:28,360 --> 00:29:32,960
that is, the address of this instruction, ra + m,

996
00:29:32,960 --> 00:29:34,760
and saves it in

997
00:29:34,760 --> 00:29:37,780
x1.

998
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:38,700
We've now agreed on this:

999
00:29:38,700 --> 00:29:40,080
it goes in x1.

1000
00:29:40,080 --> 00:29:41,920
Don't put it in any other register.

1001
00:29:41,920 --> 00:29:42,880
Then it violates the convention.

1002
00:29:42,880 --> 00:29:43,720
If that's the case, code like yours

1003
00:29:43,720 --> 00:29:45,860
may not interoperate when given to others.

1004
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:47,040
Please keep that in mind.

1005
00:29:47,040 --> 00:29:49,640
So, this way,

1006
00:29:49,640 --> 00:29:50,360
we take this

1007
00:29:50,360 --> 00:29:51,740
address of the I plus M instruction

1008
00:29:51,740 --> 00:29:53,100
and put it into this register.

1009
00:29:53,100 --> 00:29:54,140
What's stored inside it

1010
00:29:54,140 --> 00:29:56,020
is exactly the address of the I plus M instruction.

1011
00:29:56,020 --> 00:29:57,140
Please pay attention to this.

1012
00:29:57,140 --> 00:29:58,060
Then,

1013
00:29:58,060 --> 00:29:58,460
as you all know,

1014
00:29:58,460 --> 00:30:00,020
what happens next?

1015
00:30:00,020 --> 00:30:01,000
This call instruction

1016
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:03,240
is nothing more than a jump.

1017
00:30:03,240 --> 00:30:04,780
It just jumps into this call.

1018
00:30:04,780 --> 00:30:05,360
Think about it, isn't that right?

1019
00:30:05,360 --> 00:30:06,600
And then inside the call,

1020
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:07,640
it starts executing one by one

1021
00:30:07,640 --> 00:30:09,300
So our PC

1022
00:30:09,300 --> 00:30:10,540
starts executing from here

1023
00:30:10,540 --> 00:30:12,520
and then the PC just keeps executing

1024
00:30:12,520 --> 00:30:13,220
j plus 1

1025
00:30:13,220 --> 00:30:14,080
j plus 2

1026
00:30:14,080 --> 00:30:14,680
this is very simple

1027
00:30:14,680 --> 00:30:15,700
and then at the end

1028
00:30:15,700 --> 00:30:17,040
we call return

1029
00:30:17,040 --> 00:30:18,920
now when we write code

1030
00:30:18,920 --> 00:30:20,100
we just call return to go back

1031
00:30:20,100 --> 00:30:22,500
no need to write those complicated instructions anymore

1032
00:30:22,500 --> 00:30:23,200
this instruction

1033
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:24,580
what is this instruction equivalent to

1034
00:30:24,580 --> 00:30:28,640
it is equivalent to the jr instruction

1035
00:30:28,640 --> 00:30:30,300
x0 and 0

1036
00:30:30,300 --> 00:30:31,360
parenthesis x1

1037
00:30:31,360 --> 00:30:33,100
this is also a convention here

1038
00:30:33,100 --> 00:30:34,720
because the convention is x1

1039
00:30:34,720 --> 00:30:35,620
and what does that tell us

1040
00:30:35,620 --> 00:30:38,000
it jumps based on the value in here

1041
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,460
It jumps there.

1042
00:30:40,460 --> 00:30:41,520
You get what I mean, right?

1043
00:30:41,520 --> 00:30:43,400
So, based on the calculation method we just used,

1044
00:30:43,400 --> 00:30:44,740
it actually jumps to

1045
00:30:44,740 --> 00:30:46,420
the location of this instruction.

1046
00:30:46,420 --> 00:30:48,280
So it definitely jumps here.

1047
00:30:48,280 --> 00:30:50,280
Then the PC comes back,

1048
00:30:50,280 --> 00:30:52,780
and once it returns, the process is complete.

1049
00:30:52,780 --> 00:30:53,280
Think about it, isn't that right?

1050
00:30:53,280 --> 00:30:55,300
So from this example, we can see that

1051
00:30:55,300 --> 00:30:56,960
from now on, when we write code,

1052
00:30:56,960 --> 00:30:58,040
we should always use call

1053
00:30:58,040 --> 00:30:59,920
and these pseudo-instructions to implement it.

1054
00:30:59,920 --> 00:31:01,160
So why can these pseudo-instructions

1055
00:31:01,160 --> 00:31:02,540
implement this process?

1056
00:31:02,540 --> 00:31:03,440
The reason is simply that

1057
00:31:03,440 --> 00:31:05,440
they follow our programming convention,

1058
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:06,880
our programming convention—

1059
00:31:06,880 --> 00:31:09,260
the calling convention for function calls.

1060
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:16,880
This is the calling convention for function calls.

1061
00:31:16,880 --> 00:31:18,360
That is the second aspect.

1062
00:31:18,360 --> 00:31:20,980
Now, the third aspect is this:

1063
00:31:20,980 --> 00:31:23,840
Ultimately, we need to write a function.

1064
00:31:23,840 --> 00:31:25,320
So how should we write this function?

1065
00:31:25,320 --> 00:31:26,740
When we write this function,

1066
00:31:26,740 --> 00:31:27,760
there are actually some conventions to follow.

1067
00:31:27,760 --> 00:31:28,840
Please pay attention to this.

1068
00:31:28,840 --> 00:31:29,920
Of course,

1069
00:31:29,920 --> 00:31:31,400
as for this part of the content,

1070
00:31:31,400 --> 00:31:33,000
when we write C code,

1071
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:34,320
we actually don't pay much attention to it.

1072
00:31:34,320 --> 00:31:35,480
Why is that?

1073
00:31:35,480 --> 00:31:38,640
Because this part is actually handled for us by the assembler.

1074
00:31:38,640 --> 00:31:40,860
Since this follows a standard convention,

1075
00:31:40,860 --> 00:31:45,040
all assemblers will, at the beginning and end of our code,

1076
00:31:45,040 --> 00:31:46,760
insert some special instructions for us.

1077
00:31:46,760 --> 00:31:51,840
These special instructions are actually related to operations like pushing onto and popping off the stack.

1078
00:31:51,840 --> 00:31:54,500
You can't see any of this when we write C code.

1079
00:31:54,500 --> 00:31:56,400
This is the concept of automation.

1080
00:31:56,400 --> 00:32:00,520
That is, all this 'automatic' stuff doesn't really mean the program executes itself automatically.

1081
00:32:00,520 --> 00:32:05,060
It's just that these operations you perform are actually some instructions generated for you by the assembler,

1082
00:32:05,060 --> 00:32:06,280
inserted into your code.

1083
00:32:06,280 --> 00:32:07,620
You all get what I mean, right?

1084
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:08,700
Let's take a look at

1085
00:32:08,700 --> 00:32:10,500
this programming convention,

1086
00:32:10,500 --> 00:32:11,780
what exactly it does.

1087
00:32:11,780 --> 00:32:14,800
So when we write a function,

1088
00:32:14,800 --> 00:32:17,140
actually we only need to write the function body itself.

1089
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:18,140
This process of insertion,

1090
00:32:18,140 --> 00:32:19,940
actually, inserting things at the front and at the back,

1091
00:32:19,940 --> 00:32:21,700
what exactly does that do?

1092
00:32:21,700 --> 00:32:23,840
Let's look at this diagram together.

1093
00:32:23,840 --> 00:32:27,220
So first, at the beginning of the function,

1094
00:32:27,220 --> 00:32:29,040
our assembler will help us

1095
00:32:29,040 --> 00:32:30,760
insert some special push instructions

1096
00:32:30,760 --> 00:32:32,540
and some special save instructions.

1097
00:32:32,540 --> 00:32:34,540
What does it do?

1098
00:32:34,540 --> 00:32:36,840
At the beginning of the function,

1099
00:32:36,840 --> 00:32:37,900
this prologue,

1100
00:32:37,900 --> 00:32:40,580
which is also a term from specifications and the literature,

1101
00:32:40,580 --> 00:32:41,900
Just so everyone gets the idea,

1102
00:32:41,900 --> 00:32:43,820
what is it trying to express?

1103
00:32:43,820 --> 00:32:44,460
It's a bit like...

1104
00:32:44,460 --> 00:32:46,960
I actually looked this word up,

1105
00:32:46,960 --> 00:32:49,240
I pronounce it 'prologue'.

1106
00:32:49,240 --> 00:32:50,020
It's probably called 'prologue'.

1107
00:32:50,020 --> 00:32:51,200
Not entirely sure that's accurate,

1108
00:32:51,200 --> 00:32:52,460
but it's kind of like... what is it like?

1109
00:32:52,460 --> 00:32:54,900
It's like when a play is performed,

1110
00:32:54,900 --> 00:32:56,840
when the stage opens up,

1111
00:32:56,840 --> 00:32:59,860
we say there are some people who come out first to warm up the crowd.

1112
00:32:59,860 --> 00:33:00,420
You understand what I mean,

1113
00:33:00,420 --> 00:33:02,840
anyone seen this kind of thing, or whatever?

1114
00:33:02,840 --> 00:33:05,700
That is, before the main character appears,

1115
00:33:05,700 --> 00:33:06,720
there will be some extras,

1116
00:33:06,720 --> 00:33:08,200
running around in front carrying flags.

1117
00:33:08,200 --> 00:33:09,000
Get what I mean?

1118
00:33:09,000 --> 00:33:10,600
Warm up the place first.

1119
00:33:10,600 --> 00:33:12,540
This kind of thing might also be called a prologue.

1120
00:33:12,540 --> 00:33:16,740
That is, a kind of... before getting into the main content.

1121
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:19,440
The kind of process described earlier

1122
00:33:19,440 --> 00:33:21,580
Well, this process is what we call the prologue.

1123
00:33:21,580 --> 00:33:23,080
So what does it actually do?

1124
00:33:23,080 --> 00:33:25,440
First, it says it will decrease the value of SP.

1125
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:27,460
Do you know what decreasing the value of SP actually means?

1126
00:33:27,460 --> 00:33:30,040
What exactly is SP?

1127
00:33:30,040 --> 00:33:31,680
As we mentioned earlier,

1128
00:33:31,680 --> 00:33:32,940
SP is a register.

1129
00:33:32,940 --> 00:33:34,880
This register is actually x-something.

1130
00:33:34,880 --> 00:33:36,280
Is it x1?

1131
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:37,840
It's x2, right?

1132
00:33:37,840 --> 00:33:40,000
x2 is actually SP.

1133
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:41,580
SP is in fact the stack pointer.

1134
00:33:41,580 --> 00:33:43,780
So when we decrease this stack pointer,

1135
00:33:43,780 --> 00:33:44,580
think about what that means.

1136
00:33:44,580 --> 00:33:45,560
Look at the diagram on the right.

1137
00:33:45,560 --> 00:33:46,900
It's essentially taking this

1138
00:33:46,900 --> 00:33:48,680
and pushing the stack further down.

1139
00:33:48,680 --> 00:33:49,780
Do you follow what I mean?

1140
00:33:49,780 --> 00:33:50,880
After pushing the stack,

1141
00:33:50,880 --> 00:33:52,100
It actually sets up

1142
00:33:52,100 --> 00:33:53,620
You can think of it this way, conceptually

1143
00:33:53,620 --> 00:33:54,780
It's like, for this function,

1144
00:33:54,780 --> 00:33:56,240
it sets up a stack frame for the function.

1145
00:33:56,240 --> 00:33:59,200
So what is this stack frame used for?

1146
00:33:59,200 --> 00:34:00,820
It's used to save some registers,

1147
00:34:00,820 --> 00:34:03,380
and to save and define some local variables.

1148
00:34:03,380 --> 00:34:03,940
Think about it, isn't that right?

1149
00:34:03,940 --> 00:34:06,800
So decreasing the value of SP

1150
00:34:06,800 --> 00:34:08,680
is essentially setting up a stack frame for the function,

1151
00:34:08,680 --> 00:34:10,920
and then based on

1152
00:34:10,920 --> 00:34:12,840
which SAVE registers are used in this function,

1153
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:14,880
and how many local variables there are,

1154
00:34:14,880 --> 00:34:15,340
that much stack space is allocated.

1155
00:34:15,340 --> 00:34:18,340
Now, let's go back over this statement.

1156
00:34:18,340 --> 00:34:20,740
What did we mean earlier by SAVE registers?

1157
00:34:20,740 --> 00:34:22,540
SAVE registers.

1158
00:34:22,540 --> 00:34:25,140
Let's go back to that table from just now.

1159
00:34:25,140 --> 00:34:28,870
When you look at this on your own,

1160
00:34:28,870 --> 00:34:29,910
refer to this table along with it.

1161
00:34:29,910 --> 00:34:31,450
SAVE registers

1162
00:34:31,450 --> 00:34:33,230
What does that mean?

1163
00:34:33,230 --> 00:34:35,530
By convention,

1164
00:34:35,530 --> 00:34:37,130
what are save registers?

1165
00:34:37,130 --> 00:34:39,430
It means the call has to guarantee

1166
00:34:39,430 --> 00:34:40,450
the values of these registers

1167
00:34:40,450 --> 00:34:42,130
Put plainly,

1168
00:34:42,130 --> 00:34:45,730
for the caller,

1169
00:34:45,730 --> 00:34:48,130
the values in these save registers

1170
00:34:48,130 --> 00:34:50,270
before and after the call

1171
00:34:50,270 --> 00:34:52,270
must be guaranteed to remain unchanged.

1172
00:34:52,270 --> 00:34:53,090
Now, for

1173
00:34:53,090 --> 00:34:55,190
the called function,

1174
00:34:55,190 --> 00:34:56,430
how do you make sure these registers

1175
00:34:56,430 --> 00:34:58,190
stay the same before and after the call?

1176
00:34:58,190 --> 00:34:59,710
Well, the only way is to do what?

1177
00:34:59,710 --> 00:35:01,930
Don't you have to take these save registers

1178
00:35:01,930 --> 00:35:02,910
and save them?

1179
00:35:02,910 --> 00:35:03,090
Right?

1180
00:35:03,090 --> 00:35:04,330
Whether you use them or not,

1181
00:35:04,330 --> 00:35:06,470
Of course, if you optimize it better,

1182
00:35:06,470 --> 00:35:07,930
you can detect it,

1183
00:35:07,930 --> 00:35:09,590
meaning if it's not used, then don't save it.

1184
00:35:09,590 --> 00:35:11,150
And if it is used, then you save it.

1185
00:35:11,150 --> 00:35:14,390
But maybe you don't want to worry about that,

1186
00:35:14,390 --> 00:35:15,310
so no matter what,

1187
00:35:15,310 --> 00:35:15,930
following this convention,

1188
00:35:15,930 --> 00:35:17,570
you just save these save registers and that's it.

1189
00:35:17,570 --> 00:35:19,750
Then before coming out,

1190
00:35:19,750 --> 00:35:20,350
before returning,

1191
00:35:20,350 --> 00:35:22,430
just restore them again and that's it.

1192
00:35:22,430 --> 00:35:23,070
Right?

1193
00:35:23,070 --> 00:35:25,510
So about the save registers,

1194
00:35:25,510 --> 00:35:27,110
let's get a feel for what this means.

1195
00:35:27,110 --> 00:35:41,780
That is, we take the save registers,

1196
00:35:41,780 --> 00:35:44,040
based on the save registers used in the function.

1197
00:35:44,040 --> 00:35:46,420
So in principle, according to which ones you used,

1198
00:35:46,420 --> 00:35:47,940
if you used them,

1199
00:35:47,940 --> 00:35:48,880
then you save them.

1200
00:35:48,880 --> 00:35:49,440
If you didn't use them,

1201
00:35:49,440 --> 00:35:50,400
Actually, you could also skip saving them.

1202
00:35:50,400 --> 00:35:52,160
This is also something that can improve efficiency.

1203
00:35:52,160 --> 00:35:55,080
This is something for everyone to think about and get a feel for.

1204
00:35:55,080 --> 00:35:57,240
This so-called saving of the save registers,

1205
00:35:57,240 --> 00:35:58,640
what does saving it mean, exactly?

1206
00:35:58,640 --> 00:36:00,840
Now, as for local variables,

1207
00:36:00,840 --> 00:36:02,660
that is, suppose I define one inside a function,

1208
00:36:02,660 --> 00:36:04,560
a local variable called a.

1209
00:36:04,560 --> 00:36:05,880
So where does this a live?

1210
00:36:05,880 --> 00:36:06,900
Actually, it also goes on the stack.

1211
00:36:06,900 --> 00:36:08,300
You also have to allocate space for it.

1212
00:36:08,300 --> 00:36:08,480
Right?

1213
00:36:08,480 --> 00:36:10,460
Of course, you — earlier on,

1214
00:36:10,460 --> 00:36:10,980
on the right side,

1215
00:36:10,980 --> 00:36:12,220
as you move this down,

1216
00:36:12,220 --> 00:36:14,380
when laying out this stack frame,

1217
00:36:14,380 --> 00:36:15,660
it's actually been worked out ahead of time,

1218
00:36:15,660 --> 00:36:16,620
how much space this needs to allocate.

1219
00:36:16,620 --> 00:36:17,520
This is at the compilation stage,

1220
00:36:17,520 --> 00:36:18,800
and it has actually already been computed for you.

1221
00:36:18,800 --> 00:36:20,740
First, pull it down.

1222
00:36:20,740 --> 00:36:22,120
Then we start putting things into it.

1223
00:36:22,120 --> 00:36:23,860
That's essentially how it's set up.

1224
00:36:23,860 --> 00:36:25,620
Then,

1225
00:36:25,620 --> 00:36:26,840
The second step is to save the saved registers

1226
00:36:26,840 --> 00:36:27,800
onto the stack.

1227
00:36:27,800 --> 00:36:29,320
We covered this earlier.

1228
00:36:29,320 --> 00:36:30,400
Third,

1229
00:36:30,400 --> 00:36:31,780
if the function will still call

1230
00:36:31,780 --> 00:36:32,460
Pay attention here.

1231
00:36:32,460 --> 00:36:34,680
If the function will still call

1232
00:36:34,680 --> 00:36:36,020
other functions, what does that tell us?

1233
00:36:36,020 --> 00:36:39,840
It means this function is not a leaf function.

1234
00:36:39,840 --> 00:36:41,680
It will still call new functions.

1235
00:36:41,680 --> 00:36:43,980
So since it's going to call new functions,

1236
00:36:43,980 --> 00:36:46,220
why do we have to save the ra register

1237
00:36:46,220 --> 00:36:47,260
as well?

1238
00:36:47,260 --> 00:36:48,800
This is exactly what we talked about earlier,

1239
00:36:48,800 --> 00:36:50,400
the usage of the ra register.

1240
00:36:50,400 --> 00:36:57,740
Let's review it again.

1241
00:36:57,740 --> 00:37:04,100
The use of this RA register,

1242
00:37:04,100 --> 00:37:07,320
as for the RA register,

1243
00:37:07,320 --> 00:37:08,820
how did we put it just now?

1244
00:37:08,820 --> 00:37:12,180
Who is responsible during a function call

1245
00:37:12,180 --> 00:37:13,220
for maintaining these registers?

1246
00:37:13,220 --> 00:37:15,420
We said it is the caller who maintains them.

1247
00:37:15,420 --> 00:37:16,800
So the caller here,

1248
00:37:16,800 --> 00:37:17,520
that is to say,

1249
00:37:17,520 --> 00:37:20,060
who was the caller in what we just saw?

1250
00:37:20,060 --> 00:37:24,960
Let me go back again.

1251
00:37:24,960 --> 00:37:27,000
Too slow.

1252
00:37:27,000 --> 00:37:31,430
That is to say,

1253
00:37:31,430 --> 00:37:34,710
if a function also calls other functions,

1254
00:37:34,710 --> 00:37:36,970
that is, if this current function, this function,

1255
00:37:36,970 --> 00:37:38,950
also calls other functions,

1256
00:37:38,950 --> 00:37:40,670
then isn't this function the caller?

1257
00:37:40,670 --> 00:37:43,290
Do you still get what I mean?

1258
00:37:43,290 --> 00:37:44,670
Suppose this function,

1259
00:37:44,670 --> 00:37:46,330
if it also calls other functions,

1260
00:37:46,330 --> 00:37:48,510
that is, if this function also calls other functions,

1261
00:37:48,510 --> 00:37:50,550
So this function, is it the caller?

1262
00:37:50,550 --> 00:37:53,070
If it also calls other functions,

1263
00:37:53,070 --> 00:37:55,370
then this register must be saved.

1264
00:37:55,370 --> 00:37:56,210
Think about why.

1265
00:37:56,210 --> 00:37:58,330
Because we know this function,

1266
00:37:58,330 --> 00:38:02,410
suppose this function is called by another function,

1267
00:38:02,410 --> 00:38:04,350
then this function A,

1268
00:38:04,350 --> 00:38:06,510
suppose function A called this function,

1269
00:38:06,510 --> 00:38:09,690
then after function A calls function,

1270
00:38:09,690 --> 00:38:11,470
before it calls this function,

1271
00:38:11,470 --> 00:38:13,290
it must have put in the ra register,

1272
00:38:13,290 --> 00:38:15,590
its return address.

1273
00:38:15,590 --> 00:38:16,110
Think about it, right?

1274
00:38:16,110 --> 00:38:20,710
Then if this function of mine calls a new function,

1275
00:38:20,710 --> 00:38:21,570
calls C,

1276
00:38:21,570 --> 00:38:22,850
then suppose this is B,

1277
00:38:22,850 --> 00:38:25,050
suppose this B calls C again,

1278
00:38:25,050 --> 00:38:26,370
then when calling C,

1279
00:38:26,370 --> 00:38:27,510
we know that when calling C,

1280
00:38:27,510 --> 00:38:30,410
it will definitely overwrite the value in the ra register

1281
00:38:30,410 --> 00:38:31,430
change it to its return address

1282
00:38:31,430 --> 00:38:31,950
think about it, right?

1283
00:38:31,950 --> 00:38:34,550
Now, if it modifies this value,

1284
00:38:34,550 --> 00:38:37,350
then the next time it returns and wants to go back to A,

1285
00:38:37,350 --> 00:38:38,870
it won't be able to get back.

1286
00:38:38,870 --> 00:38:40,110
That's the problem we'd run into.

1287
00:38:40,110 --> 00:38:42,090
So, given that this is the case,

1288
00:38:42,090 --> 00:38:44,530
since I've determined that this function,

1289
00:38:44,530 --> 00:38:45,870
if B is going to call C,

1290
00:38:45,870 --> 00:38:48,750
then I need to take the original value in ra,

1291
00:38:48,750 --> 00:38:50,750
that is, the original return address of A,

1292
00:38:50,750 --> 00:38:51,530
and do what with it?

1293
00:38:51,530 --> 00:38:53,010
Save it.

1294
00:38:53,010 --> 00:38:57,630
Before my function returns,

1295
00:38:57,630 --> 00:38:59,390
I just restore it, and that's it.

1296
00:38:59,390 --> 00:39:00,890
That's the idea.

1297
00:39:00,890 --> 00:39:05,390
So if this function is going to call other functions,

1298
00:39:05,390 --> 00:39:07,270
we need to take the value of ra,

1299
00:39:07,270 --> 00:39:09,450
what's actually stored in here is essentially,

1300
00:39:09,450 --> 00:39:11,490
the return address value of function A.

1301
00:39:11,490 --> 00:39:13,130
the value of this A function

1302
00:39:13,130 --> 00:39:15,490
we save it

1303
00:39:15,490 --> 00:39:17,030
and then later return it

1304
00:39:17,530 --> 00:39:18,810
and just restore it, and that's it

1305
00:39:20,610 --> 00:39:21,370
So this is

1306
00:39:21,630 --> 00:39:25,210
basically, before our function actually executes,

1307
00:39:25,470 --> 00:39:26,490
there's actually a lot of work to be done.

1308
00:39:27,010 --> 00:39:29,570
And this work is also part of our convention.

1309
00:39:31,870 --> 00:39:34,950
Now, next, let's look at the function epilogue.

1310
00:39:35,210 --> 00:39:37,510
The epilogue is basically the reverse of this process.

1311
00:39:38,530 --> 00:39:39,290
A reverse operation.

1312
00:39:39,550 --> 00:39:42,890
The first step of the reverse operation is to restore what was temporarily saved.

1313
00:39:43,130 --> 00:39:44,670
Because we saved these things

1314
00:39:44,670 --> 00:39:45,850
earlier,

1315
00:39:45,850 --> 00:39:47,410
and since we saved them,

1316
00:39:47,410 --> 00:39:48,290
we restore them.

1317
00:39:48,290 --> 00:39:49,030
After restoring them,

1318
00:39:49,030 --> 00:39:51,770
with respect to the caller of this function,

1319
00:39:51,770 --> 00:39:52,610
A,

1320
00:39:52,610 --> 00:39:54,090
it's as if nothing has changed.

1321
00:39:54,090 --> 00:39:54,530
Right?

1322
00:39:54,530 --> 00:39:56,830
And at the same time, if needed,

1323
00:39:56,830 --> 00:39:58,810
restore the saved ra from the stack.

1324
00:39:58,810 --> 00:40:00,890
And what we mean by "if needed" is exactly this,

1325
00:40:00,890 --> 00:40:02,570
that is, if there is a call,

1326
00:40:02,570 --> 00:40:03,470
if there was no call,

1327
00:40:03,470 --> 00:40:04,450
then actually nothing gets saved.

1328
00:40:04,450 --> 00:40:06,170
If nothing is saved, then there is no need to restore it.

1329
00:40:06,170 --> 00:40:09,030
So these two things correspond to each other.

1330
00:40:09,030 --> 00:40:10,330
Then the last step,

1331
00:40:10,330 --> 00:40:12,130
step three, increase sp.

1332
00:40:12,130 --> 00:40:13,550
Now, what we mean by increasing sp,

1333
00:40:13,550 --> 00:40:14,230
you can imagine,

1334
00:40:14,230 --> 00:40:16,030
is that we are about to pop.

1335
00:40:16,030 --> 00:40:17,070
We are going to deallocate the stack frame.

1336
00:40:17,070 --> 00:40:19,570
Then the call uses ret to return.

1337
00:40:19,570 --> 00:40:20,950
This ret is exactly what we talked about earlier,

1338
00:40:20,950 --> 00:40:22,050
a standard function call.

1339
00:40:22,050 --> 00:40:28,480
And that finally achieves this kind of effect.

1340
00:40:28,480 --> 00:40:30,920
So this involves

1341
00:40:30,920 --> 00:40:32,380
That is to say, the called function,

1342
00:40:32,380 --> 00:40:33,560
that is, this function of ours,

1343
00:40:33,560 --> 00:40:36,380
say A calls B,

1344
00:40:36,380 --> 00:40:37,560
calls a function,

1345
00:40:37,560 --> 00:40:39,860
then inside this function,

1346
00:40:39,860 --> 00:40:41,220
actually, around the function body,

1347
00:40:41,220 --> 00:40:42,960
before the function body and after the function body,

1348
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:44,480
there are some standard operations

1349
00:40:44,480 --> 00:40:45,680
that we need to do.

1350
00:40:45,680 --> 00:40:47,160
This part of the work,

1351
00:40:47,160 --> 00:40:48,060
we normally don't see it,

1352
00:40:48,060 --> 00:40:50,300
but that doesn't mean it doesn't exist.

1353
00:40:50,300 --> 00:40:52,820
It's actually done for us by the compiler.

1354
00:40:52,820 --> 00:40:53,260
But,

1355
00:40:53,260 --> 00:40:54,280
today,

1356
00:40:54,280 --> 00:40:56,000
we're going to try writing it ourselves.

1357
00:40:56,000 --> 00:40:58,920
Suppose you had to implement it — how would you do it?

1358
00:40:58,920 --> 00:41:02,500
to deepen your impression of this process.

1359
00:41:02,500 --> 00:41:06,560
So basically, that covers everything about function calling conventions,

1360
00:41:06,560 --> 00:41:08,880
I've now introduced it all to you.

1361
00:41:08,880 --> 00:41:10,600
So let's go through some exercises

1362
00:41:10,600 --> 00:41:16,450
to see how this actually works in practice.

1363
00:41:16,450 --> 00:41:19,570
Next, we'll walk you through

1364
00:41:19,570 --> 00:41:20,770
a quick look at

1365
00:41:20,770 --> 00:41:21,830
these two examples here.

1366
00:41:21,830 --> 00:41:24,190
One is ccleaf,

1367
00:41:24,190 --> 00:41:26,350
and the other is called ccnested.

1368
00:41:26,350 --> 00:41:27,310
Now, this one,

1369
00:41:27,310 --> 00:41:28,170
basically, leaf,

1370
00:41:28,170 --> 00:41:30,510
means this is a leaf call,

1371
00:41:30,510 --> 00:41:31,490
that is, a leaf call,

1372
00:41:31,490 --> 00:41:33,770
the concept of a leaf call.

1373
00:41:33,770 --> 00:41:35,610
As for nested,

1374
00:41:35,610 --> 00:41:36,430
it means a

1375
00:41:36,430 --> 00:41:38,950
non-leaf call, essentially.

1376
00:41:38,950 --> 00:41:43,930
Now, ccleaf,

1377
00:41:43,930 --> 00:41:44,430
that is to say,

1378
00:41:44,430 --> 00:41:46,530
what exactly is a leaf call?

1379
00:41:46,530 --> 00:41:47,590
Let's take a look at this example.

1380
00:41:47,590 --> 00:41:48,830
If we write it in C,

1381
00:41:48,830 --> 00:41:49,350
That is to say,

1382
00:41:49,350 --> 00:41:51,310
suppose you look at this start function,

1383
00:41:51,310 --> 00:41:52,750
it calls this square function,

1384
00:41:52,750 --> 00:41:53,990
just to compute a square,

1385
00:41:53,990 --> 00:41:55,570
a times a.

1386
00:41:55,570 --> 00:41:58,830
So after it calls this square function,

1387
00:41:58,830 --> 00:42:00,810
this function won't call anything else.

1388
00:42:00,810 --> 00:42:03,750
In other words, after this call,

1389
00:42:03,750 --> 00:42:04,590
this function,

1390
00:42:04,590 --> 00:42:06,830
the square function, won't call any new functions.

1391
00:42:06,830 --> 00:42:07,590
Of course, isn't that right?

1392
00:42:07,590 --> 00:42:09,750
This kind is called a leaf call,

1393
00:42:09,750 --> 00:42:11,050
or a pseudo-call.

1394
00:42:11,050 --> 00:42:13,210
Now let's take a look.

1395
00:42:13,210 --> 00:42:15,790
Now, following this calling convention,

1396
00:42:15,790 --> 00:42:17,050
how do we write this?

